# .gitattributes — syntax-aware merge for this repo's hotspot file types.
#
# WHY: ~20 branch worktrees land onto the same `staging/local`, so the same
# yaml/json/mjs/md files are edited in parallel constantly. mergiraf parses both
# sides with tree-sitter and merges at the syntax-tree level, so two branches
# that add different keys to the same object, or different tests to the same
# file, merge cleanly instead of producing a textual conflict a human (or an
# LLM resolver) has to settle.
#
# Per-path merge drivers apply to LOCAL merges/rebases only — GitHub's web merge
# ignores .gitattributes. That is exactly our case: `scripts/land-branch.sh`,
# `scripts/integrate-branch.sh`, and `stories/land` all rebase locally.
#
# --- mergiraf: syntax-aware merge driver -----------------------------------
#
# Everything in the block below is generated verbatim by
#
#     mergiraf languages --gitattributes
#
# (mergiraf 0.18.0), so regenerating it after an upgrade is a mechanical diff.
# Do not hand-edit individual lines; re-run the command instead.
#
# The driver itself is NOT defined by this file. A `merge=mergiraf` attribute is
# inert unless `merge.mergiraf.driver` exists in the clone's git config;
# `scripts/setup.sh` (`make setup`) installs the binary and writes that config.
# Without it git silently falls back to its normal line merge, so committing
# these rules is safe everywhere — including CI, which never merges.
#
# ORDERING IS LOAD-BEARING: the last matching line wins. Keep the mergiraf block
# FIRST so repo-specific overrides below can win. Verify an override with:
#
#     git check-attr merge -- <path>
#
# Not covered: `.pongo` (Kitsoki view templates) and `.vue` have no mergiraf
# grammar, so those 8 files stay on git's line merge.

